<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">High Commission Territories</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The High Commission Territories were the three British dependencies of Basutoland, the Bechuanaland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>, governed through the British High Commissioner for southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> long hoped to absorb them, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> refused, and they became independent as Lesotho and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> in 1966 and Eswatini in 1968.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/high-commission-territories.jpg" alt="High Commission Territories" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Great Brightstar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_High_Commissioner_for_Southern_Africa_(1907%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Second Boer War Britain took over, administering the territory first through the Transvaal from 1903 and from 1906 as a <b>High Commission territory</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Cape attempts to disarm the Basotho caused the Gun War of 1880-1881, which the Basotho effectively won, and in 1884 Britain resumed direct rule as a <b>High Commission</b> territory from Maseru.</blockquote><a href="/state/basutoland">Basutoland</a></li></ul></section>
